892:## PaymasterFactory CREATE2 salt bound to the operator (Daybreak 053117cc DB-d-M3, PaymasterFactory-1.1.0, 2026-11-18)
894:**Finding (Medium, reachable in 5.5.0).** `deployPaymasterDeterministic` used the caller's salt unchanged. Anyone who copied a victim's salt could deploy first and land a clone at the victim's predicted address. That clone's owner is the attacker, because `_initAndVerify` requires owner == msg.sender. The attacker could then `withdrawTo` any EntryPoint deposit the victim had already made to that address. This was reproduced on main `053117cc` with a real EntryPoint v0.7: a 1 ETH prefund was taken.
909:## Completed guardian exit retires the seat (Daybreak 053117cc M-01, BLSAggregator-4.14.2, 2026-11-17)
916:It needed no new PoP, no `addValidator` and no owner approval. Both halves were reproduced on main `053117cc` (real Registry, DVTValidator and keys, Prague).
936:## Daybreak 053117cc batch (c): DB-c-M1 exit hook and L-01 oracle timestamps (SuperPaymaster-5.5.3, Staking-4.2.1, 2026-11-18)
953:- **Sepolia:** keeps GTokenStaking `0x472297B557c1d0F030f281a5Bb8A535f6c5AB65e` (Staking-4.2.0), because GTokenStaking is not upgradeable. That runtime is byte-identical to the `053117cc` build with immutables masked, metadata hash included. The only difference from the tag tree is this hook plus the version string.
982:It could re-approve staking and pay the 30 GToken ticket through the permissionless
1024:140 locked in stakes and 2059 transferred to the recipient. All 74 GToken in
1025:entry tickets went back to the deployer as the then-current staking treasury.

